{
 "id": "REL-PUB-01",
 "criterion": "Cargo publish runs `cargo publish --manifest-path <pkg>/Cargo.toml --locked` from the workspace root, adding `--registry <key>` for registries other than crates.io. It runs only after the on-disk version (resolving `version.workspace = true`) equals the planned version and fails with an on-disk version drift error otherwise."
}
{
 "id": "REL-PUB-02",
 "criterion": "npm publish picks its tool from the workspace that owns the package and runs there. The owner is the nearest directory at or above the package whose `package.json` declares `workspaces` or that has a `pnpm-workspace.yaml`, else for a package no workspace declares the nearest directory with a lockfile, else the root; a lockfile beside a package never moves it out of a declared workspace. The `npm view` observation and a publish by directory run in that same directory, so one `.npmrc` governs what is looked for and what is published. A marker that cannot be inspected is an error, not `npm`. `pnpm-workspace.yaml` alone means pnpm. The tools: `pnpm publish --filter=<name> --no-git-checks`, `yarn workspace <name> npm publish`, `bun publish` in the package directory, and otherwise `npm publish --workspace=<name>`. It appends `--tag <label>` for prerelease versions, where the label is the lowercase letters that start the first prerelease identifier (`1.0.0-beta.2` publishes under `beta`), or `next` when there are none or the word is `latest`, and `--access` and `--registry` when set. A stable version takes the registry default tag."
}
{
 "id": "REL-PUB-03",
 "criterion": "A scoped npm package (`@scope/name`) with no configured access publishes with `--access public`. A configured access value always wins, and an unscoped package with no configured access gets no `--access` flag."
}
{
 "id": "REL-PUB-04",
 "criterion": "PyPI publish builds with `python3 -m build --sdist --wheel --outdir <temporary directory>` in the package directory, falling back to `python` and failing with E170 when neither is on PATH, and fails with E306 when the build produced no wheel or no sdist for the version. It then runs `twine upload` naming only the built files the index lacks, with `--repository-url` for a configured upload endpoint (a registry's `publish-url`, else its `url`) and never `--skip-existing`. One build serves the preflight and the publish of a run. Every build runs with `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH` set to the release commit's committer time. `release build-python` runs the same build for each Python publish of a plan into `--artifact-dir`."
}
{
 "id": "REL-PUB-05",
 "criterion": "A `publishConfig.registry` in package.json that does not match the `url` of an npm-kind `[registries]` entry fails with E120. A registry URL that is not `https`, or has userinfo, a query or a fragment, fails with E126. An `origin` push URL that is not HTTPS or `git@` SSH, or that carries credentials, fails with E132. All three fail before any effect.",
 "is_error_case": true
}
{
 "id": "REL-PUB-06",
 "criterion": "Cargo observation runs `cargo --config net.retry=0 info NAME@VERSION --registry <name>` from the workspace root, passing `crates-io` for the default. Exit 0 with a matching `version:` line means present, exit 101 with ``could not find `NAME@VERSION` `` means absent, and anything else is indeterminate."
}
{
 "id": "REL-PUB-07",
 "criterion": "npm observation runs `npm view NAME@VERSION --json --fetch-retries=0`, with `--registry` when configured. Exit 0 with output means present. A failure whose npm error code is E404, read from the JSON error body on stdout or else from the `code` line on stderr, means absent. Exit 0 with no output, and any other failure, leave the observation indeterminate and are never read as absence."
}
{
 "id": "REL-PUB-08",
 "criterion": "PyPI observation fetches `<index>/<normalized-name>/` (the registry's `url`, default `https://pypi.org/simple`; never its `publish-url`) with `curl` and `Accept: application/vnd.pypi.simple.v1+json`. In a 200 JSON body, any matching yanked file means absent; otherwise at least one matching non-yanked file means present. That version-level read serves every use outside an effect. Before a PyPI effect, `release execute` proceeds without a build when the index has no file of the version. Otherwise it builds, and treats the release as already satisfied only when every built file is on the index by filename and not yanked. A built file the index holds yanked is a remote conflict, and a file the index holds that the build did not produce is reported with a warning and not checked. After an upload, confirmation waits for every built file to be visible, and a file this run uploaded must carry the sha256 the index serves for it, or the run fails with E307. A file from an earlier attempt is matched by name, not by digest, unless the artifact manifest seals the release's files: then the run builds nothing, uploads those exact files, compares the sha256 of every file the index holds under a sealed name, and fails with E307 before any upload when one differs. A 404 or no matching file means absent, and a non-JSON body or other non-retryable status is indeterminate. Transient HTTP statuses use the observation retry policy."
}
{
 "id": "REL-PUB-09",
 "criterion": "After a publish command reports success, or says the version already exists, Callisto re-observes the registry and treats \"absent\" or an unreachable registry as not yet propagated, waiting within the registry's confirmation window: `confirm-timeout` on its `[registries]` entry (1 s to 1 h, written as `90s`, `6m` or `1h`), else 6 minutes for npm and PyPI and 2 minutes for Cargo. Looks are spaced 15 s, 45 s, 2 minutes, then 3 minutes apart. A publish whose version the registry never shows in that window fails with E157, and no receipt is written.",
 "is_error_case": true
}
{
 "id": "REL-PUB-10",
 "criterion": "Read-only observations before an effect (registry query, GitHub release lookup, `git ls-remote`) run at most 5 attempts with exponential backoff from 2 s, capped at 300 s. HTTP 429, 5xx, rate-limited 403 and failed commands are retried, and an observation still indeterminate fails with E176 before that operation's effect.",
 "is_error_case": true
}
{
 "id": "REL-PUB-11",
 "criterion": "A publish command that fails with auth, rate-limit or other registry output fails the run with E163, and the error carries the last 8 lines of the command's standard output followed by its standard error. A publish run also echoes its standard output to the live log, on stderr. A failing git or gh effect command fails with E164. No effect command is re-issued automatically. Tool output shown in these errors, in the live stderr echo and in JSON errors has credentials masked as `[REDACTED]`: the values of the known credential variables, of `CARGO_REGISTRIES_*_TOKEN`, and of any variable named `*_TOKEN`, `*_SECRET` or `*_PASSWORD` whose value is at least 8 characters, URL userinfo, `npm_`, `ghp_`-family, `github_pat_` and `pypi-` tokens, `Bearer` values, and `.npmrc` `_authToken`, `_auth` and `_password` values.",
 "is_error_case": true
}
{
 "id": "REL-PUB-12",
 "criterion": "A `pypi` `[registries]` entry may set `publish-url`, the endpoint Twine uploads to when the index at `url` is not also the upload route. Both endpoints follow the registry URL rules of REL-PUB-05 and both enter the registry binding, so changing either makes an earlier intent stale. A package-level `publish-to` index names both routes and overrides the entry's `publish-url`. A `publish-url` on a registry that is not `pypi` is rejected when the configuration loads."
}
{
 "id": "REL-PUB-13",
 "criterion": "A registry entry may set `publish-timeout`, the longest one publish command (`cargo publish`, `npm publish`, `twine upload`) may run, as a whole number with `s`, `m` or `h` from 1 s to 1 h; the default is 15 minutes. A command that runs longer is stopped and fails with E025. The value does not enter the registry binding, so changing it does not make an earlier intent stale. A value outside that form or range is rejected when the configuration loads.",
 "is_error_case": true
}
{
 "id": "REL-PUB-14",
 "criterion": "When a Cargo or npm registry publish is adopted because the exact version is already published, and the registry entry names a repository whose host, owner and name differ from the `origin` remote's (compared case-insensitively, ignoring `.git`, `git+` and URL form), Callisto prints a warning on stderr naming both repositories. The run still adopts the version. No warning is printed when the registry names no repository, when either value cannot be read as a repository, or when they match. PyPI is not checked. When the package has no tag in this repository (a first release) and the registry holds the version under a differing repository, `release` and `release --dry-run` fail with E322 before anything else, because the version is another project's; tagging the commit that set the version by hand makes the package no longer a first release."
}
{
 "id": "REL-PUB-15",
 "criterion": "A `[registries.<name>]` entry must set `kind` to `cargo`, `npm` or `pypi`, except the built-in keys `cratesIo`, `npm` and `pypi`, which default to their own kind. A custom registry without `kind` is rejected when the configuration loads with E321, instead of being read as an npm registry.",
 "is_error_case": true
}
{
 "id": "REL-PUB-16",
 "criterion": "A package this repository has never tagged that publishes to crates.io must set `description` and either `license` or `license-file` in its `Cargo.toml`, directly or from `[workspace.package]`; an empty value counts as unset. Otherwise `release` and `release --dry-run` stop with E323 naming the package and the missing field, before any publish, tag or push.",
 "is_error_case": true
}
{
 "id": "REL-PUB-17",
 "criterion": "Every Callisto-launched child other than Cargo has `CARGO_REGISTRIES_*_TOKEN` variables removed, matched without case sensitivity, whether inherited or supplied to a publisher subprocess. This covers ordinary, timeout, filtered, stdin, quiet and publisher routes. Cargo keeps named registry tokens for its native authentication."
}

